WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.047
Welcome to Module Nine: Production Monitoring and Reliability.

2
00:00:04.377 --> 00:00:08.871
Your model is deployed; now it has to keep working for real users, every day.

3
00:00:09.671 --> 00:00:14.187
Picture a phone company's support bot, the morning after a big marketing campaign.

4
00:00:14.517 --> 00:00:17.432
Traffic triples, and customers say the bot feels slow.

5
00:00:18.232 --> 00:00:22.811
The dashboard shows TTFT jumping from under half a second to three seconds.

6
00:00:23.141 --> 00:00:26.547
Yet time per output token is flat, and errors haven't moved.

7
00:00:27.347 --> 00:00:31.479
That pattern points at the queue: requests wait before prefill even starts.

8
00:00:31.809 --> 00:00:35.001
Queue depth had been climbing for twenty minutes, with no alert.

9
00:00:35.801 --> 00:00:38.994
They scale out, and two weeks later a quieter problem appears.

10
00:00:39.324 --> 00:00:43.093
Every dashboard is green, yet customer satisfaction keeps falling.

11
00:00:43.893 --> 00:00:47.939
A new plan has launched, and users ask questions the bot has never seen.

12
00:00:48.269 --> 00:00:52.549
GPU metrics can't see wrong answers; drift and quality checks can.

13
00:00:53.349 --> 00:00:56.542
This module teaches you to catch both kinds of failure early.

14
00:00:56.872 --> 00:01:00.619
Lesson one starts with the golden signals of LLM serving.

15
00:01:01.419 --> 00:01:06.681
You'll track TTFT, time per token, queue depth, KV-cache use and errors.

16
00:01:07.011 --> 00:01:10.417
Always as percentiles, because averages hide the slow tail.

17
00:01:11.217 --> 00:01:13.742
Prometheus scrapes vLLM, Triton,

18
00:01:13.792 --> 00:01:18.570
NIM and the DCGM exporter, and Grafana draws the dashboards.

19
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:24.183
Alert on symptoms users feel, then diagnose with causes like GPU Xid errors.

20
00:01:24.983 --> 00:01:27.920
Logs and traces show which request failed, and where.

21
00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:31.661
OpenTelemetry follows it through retriever and model,

22
00:01:31.711 --> 00:01:34.664
and PII is redacted before anything is stored.

23
00:01:35.464 --> 00:01:38.891
Then you'll detect drift in prompt length, topics and embeddings.

24
00:01:39.221 --> 00:01:44.910
An LLM judge scores a small sample of live answers, to catch silent quality drops.

25
00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:48.028
Lesson two covers the model lifecycle.

26
00:01:48.358 --> 00:01:51.455
A version bundles weights, tokenizer, chat template,

27
00:01:51.505 --> 00:01:54.239
prompts and generation settings in a registry.

28
00:01:55.039 --> 00:01:58.573
Every candidate must pass the same regression suite as production.

29
00:01:58.903 --> 00:02:02.927
Then it rolls out gradually: shadow traffic first, then a small canary.

30
00:02:03.727 --> 00:02:05.235
If the canary's scores slip,

31
00:02:05.285 --> 00:02:08.349
traffic swings back to the previous version, still warm.

32
00:02:08.679 --> 00:02:11.168
Rollback should be fast, rehearsed and boring.

33
00:02:11.968 --> 00:02:16.675
Quality drops, drift and user feedback trigger a reproducible retraining pipeline.

34
00:02:17.005 --> 00:02:20.731
But stale facts are fixed by updating the RAG index, not the weights.

35
00:02:21.531 --> 00:02:25.285
By the end, you'll build LLM dashboards with SLO alerts,

36
00:02:25.335 --> 00:02:28.137
catch drift, and ship new versions safely.

37
00:02:28.467 --> 00:02:31.084
Let's begin with lesson one: observability.
